"oldValue": "The council of europe’s anti-corruption monitoring body Headquartered in Palace of Europe. Founded 1999.", "newValue": "The Group of States Against Corruption (GRECO) serves as an essential monitoring body established in 1999 under the auspices of the Council of Europe, headquartered in the Palace of Europe in Strasbourg, France. Its formation was a direct response to the growing recognition of corruption as a pervasive threat to democratic governance, economic stability, and social justice across Europe and beyond. GRECO's mission is to enhance the capacity of its member states to combat corruption through a rigorous peer-review mechanism and compliance assessments. This body comprises 50 member states, including all 47 Council of Europe member states and the United States, underlining its significance in global anti-corruption efforts.

Since its inception, GRECO has conducted numerous evaluations, producing over 20 rounds of reports assessing the anti-corruption measures of its member states. These evaluations cover various domains, including the judiciary, law enforcement, and public officials' conduct, leading to tangible improvements in national laws and practices. For instance, in its 2018 evaluation cycle, GRECO highlighted the need for enhanced transparency in political financing, which resulted in legislative reforms in several countries, demonstrating the organization's impactful oversight.

The legacy of GRECO lies in its contribution to the international discourse on corruption and governance, shaping how member states approach anti-corruption strategies. As a result of GRECO's influence, many countries have adopted best practices and strengthened legal frameworks. Furthermore, GRECO's reports have become key references for civil society organizations and watchdogs, illustrating the interdependence of global governance and accountability mechanisms."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T16:58:49Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#29351522391", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Organizational need and institutional development"], "newValue": ["The rise of corruption scandals in European nations during the late 20th century highlighted the need for collective action.", "The establishment of the Council of Europe as a platform for promoting human rights and democratic governance created a foundation for GRECO's formation.", "The globalization of political and economic relations in the post-Cold War era made corruption a transnational issue requiring international cooperation."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T16:58:49Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#29351522391", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Shaped institutional landscape and community development"], "newValue": ["GRECO's evaluations have led to significant legislative reforms in over 30 member states, enhancing anti-corruption measures.", "The organization has fostered a culture of accountability, compelling nations to adopt best practices in governance.", "GRECO's influence has spurred the establishment of similar anti-corruption bodies in non-member countries, amplifying its global impact."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T16:58:49Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#29351522391", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "group-of-states-against-corruption", "sourceName": "Group of States Against Corruption", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "era-post-